\title{mysterymodel: Opinionated Workflow for Clinical Prediction Models}
\description{
An orchestration layer for clinical prediction modelling. Established engines do the statistics: 'rms' for regression, internal validation, calibration and nomograms, the 'easystats' packages for diagnostics and interpretation, and 'tidymodels' for resampling and model comparison. This package supplies the workflow holding them together and the methodological safeguards that keep a published model honest. It makes no model-selection decision on the analyst's behalf and hides none.
}
\details{
An orchestration layer, not an estimator. \code{rms} fits the regression,
resamples it and draws the nomogram; \code{pmsampsize} answers the sample-size
question; \code{pROC} supplies the interval on the c-statistic. What this package
adds is the workflow holding them together and the disclosures that keep a
published model honest.
}
\section{The order things happen in}{


Each step reads the object the one before it returned, so no step has to
guess and no two steps can quietly disagree about the cohort.
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=mm_project]{mm_project()}} records the analysis plan and refuses combinations that
cannot be meant. Nothing is fitted.
\item \code{\link[=mm_clean]{mm_clean()}} applies eligibility rules and returns a ledger that
reconciles: rows in equals rows out plus every exclusion, or it errors.
\item \code{\link[=mm_eda]{mm_eda()}} describes the cohort with the denominator each percentage is
taken over and the missing count beside it.
\item \code{\link[=mm_model]{mm_model()}} fits with \code{\link[rms:lrm]{rms::lrm()}}, decides the modelling rows once,
scans for a predictor that carries the outcome, and reports whether the
sample supports the parameters spent.
\item \code{\link[=mm_validate]{mm_validate()}} returns one row per basis.
\item \code{\link[=mm_predict]{mm_predict()}} scores new rows against the schema recorded at fit time.
\item \code{\link[=mm_nomogram]{mm_nomogram()}} draws the nomogram and proves it agrees with
\code{\link[=mm_predict]{mm_predict()}} before it will return one.
}

\code{\link[=mm_synthetic_cohort]{mm_synthetic_cohort()}} generates a cohort with known truth, which is what
every example and article here runs on: the public repository carries no
patient data.
}

\section{What the package will not do quietly}{


The recurring decision is that a number which cannot be what it claims to
be is refused or disclosed, never corrected into something plausible. A
silent correction produces a figure a reader will believe.
\itemize{
\item A predictor that reproduces the outcome is refused, because a model given
its own outcome reports near-perfect discrimination, which reads as
success. Perfect separation that is not duplication is reported instead,
because in a small cohort it can be real.
\item Apparent, bootstrap-corrected and held-out performance are separate rows
with separate notes. Apparent calibration is withheld, because scoring an
unpenalized fit on its own rows forces the intercept to zero and the
slope to one.
\item A c-statistic's interval names its method and the event and non-event
counts it rests on. One that cannot be computed is \code{NA} with the reason;
one of zero width says so rather than reading as precision.
\item Rows dropped for incompleteness are counted, attributed to the variable
that cost them, and reported as a complete-case assumption rather than a
finding.
\item The fitted model carries no copy of the cohort, so a saved model cannot
carry patient identifiers somewhere the data was never meant to go.
}
}

\section{Where the reasoning is written down}{


\code{inst/decisions.md} holds the decision log, one record per settled question,
each with the evidence behind it. \code{inst/capability-matrix.md} records which
helpers in the surrounding ecosystem actually work on an \code{rms} fit, and is
regenerated by \code{data-raw/capability_spike.R}.
}

